1
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:11,120
And take a step back and look at it.

2
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:24,300
Anyone who's broke, come to me.

3
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,960
I've got nothing either, but don't worry.

4
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:32,820
Look at the blue sky,

5
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:36,300
white clouds.

6
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:42,740
Somehow, it'll all work out eventually.

7
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:59,900
Anyone without a job, come to me.

8
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:04,560
I've got none either, so don't worry.

9
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:55,040
The express Azemichi-gō runs through the
rice paddies as far as the eye can see.

10
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:00,180
The scenery reflected in the train window brings
back the heart that we modern people have forgotten.

11
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:02,060
It seems to bring back what we'd lost.

12
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,680
Travelling really is wonderful, isn't it.

13
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:08,420
Hey, Reiko.

14
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:08,840
Yes?

15
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,940
What is it, Ryotsu? What are you up to now?

16
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:17,280
Getting into travel mode with just one can of beer,
you really are cheap.

17
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:21,060
Right, and on top of that it's a local train,
nothing like the express.

18
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:23,800
I've been on this train for ages,
so I was bored.

19
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,304
This isn't just any trip,

20
00:02:25,354 --> 00:02:28,040
we're going to look for Reiko's memories.

21
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:30,480
A journey to find memories, how romantic.

22
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:39,440
What a nostalgic view.

23
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:45,240
Nostalgic?
But senpai, you were born in Tokyo, right?

24
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:47,260
I've always lived in Asakusa, though.

25
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:50,980
Somehow, when I think of my hometown,
I picture scenery like this.

26
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:52,060
That's true.

27
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,220
This must be what Japan's
countryside feels like.

28
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:59,220
Just looking at it, you can almost feel
the warm sunlight and smell the grass.

29
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,660
Ryo-chan, you're such a poet.
Let's see, let's see.

30
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:06,160
I'm not like a poet, I am a poet.

31
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,480
What's wrong?

32
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:12,860
I've seen this view before,
back when I was in elementary school.

33
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,820
Don't be stupid.
This is Japanese scenery, you know.

34
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:19,360
Reiko, you were born in France
and raised in Europe, right?

35
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:22,360
You couldn't have been in Japan during elementary school,
could you?

36
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:26,040
But this scenery really is familiar.

37
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:29,060
Yes, I remember now.

38
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:32,820
When I started elementary school,
I came to Japan just once.

39
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:34,600
I must have seen it then.

40
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:35,720
Really?

41
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:43,160
I came to Japan as a new ambassador,
delivering paintings to elementary schools.

42
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,380
I was moved by the scenery of Japan,
which I was seeing for the first time.

43
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:50,980
Is this the place?

44
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:55,120
Not sure.
Scenery like this is everywhere in Japan.

45
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:56,440
It's definitely here.

46
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:57,980
There's no doubt about it.

47
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,240
But there was something more precious,
a memory I have here.

48
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,080
I wonder what it was.

49
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:06,700
Hey, Ryo-chan,
can you take me to this place?

50
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:08,240
Why should I?

51
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:10,980
You said you hadn't taken your summer vacation yet,
didn't you?

52
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:12,340
I haven't either.

53
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:14,340
Ryo-chan, you're good at finding things like this,
aren't you?

54
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:17,960
Why should I have to do something
like that just because of that?

55
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:19,700
The other day, from Ryo-chan's trash bin,

56
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,160
a shard of the Chief's
precious teacup turned up.

57
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:23,600
Gulp!

58
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:25,480
You said you were going
to have a bonfire out back,

59
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:27,540
so who was it that was
burning the reprimand letter?

60
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:29,380
Fine, I'll come along.

61
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,220
Thanks, Ryo-chan.

62
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:33,780
Damn it,
taking advantage of my weakness like that.

63
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:35,840
But even if we search for it,

64
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:42,480
I'll ask around. Got it, it's terribly rural.
A day trip is out of the question.

65
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:53,120
For the countryside, we need a helper who knows the countryside well.
I already asked Nakagawa downstairs to reach out to Miss Mamushi, the queen of the countryside.

66
00:04:53,120 --> 00:05:01,580
It's all the same, right? Besides, what, it's
like you're taking me on a trip or something.

67
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:03,020
Yeah, I'm treating.

68
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,700
What's with the high and mighty
attitude on this slow train?

69
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:09,180
How old are you?
These days they call it a local train.

70
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:14,980
Is this mountain road really okay?

71
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:18,540
Are we really on the right road?

72
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:21,040
What? I can't hear you.

73
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:24,620
The cicadas are so loud
I can't hear a thing.

74
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:28,115
Well, whatever.
I'll leave the navigation to Komachi,

75
00:05:28,165 --> 00:05:29,680
I came here for my real purpose,

76
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:32,320
after all, I set out to

77
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:35,820
catch a giant stag beetle that
sells for a high price on this chance

78
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:37,540
and rake in a fortune.

79
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:39,460
I'm more energetic than my memories.

80
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:44,880
What, it suddenly went quiet.

81
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,140
Just as I thought.

82
00:05:46,140 --> 00:05:48,560
I figured you wouldn't
move for nothing, though.

83
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:50,960
Ryo-chan, you were saying that out loud.

84
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:53,440
Hahaha!

85
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,700
We've come out into an open area.

86
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:57,400
See, it was a shortcut, wasn't it?

87
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,180
Instinct is what matters in these things,
isn't it?

88
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:00,880
Just like the queen of the countryside.

89
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:19,580
I wonder if it's because the country cicadas fly a long
way from forest to forest and get a lot of exercise.

90
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:23,180
I've told you I don't know,
you're so persistent.

91
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:25,540
I wonder if their calls have dialects too.

92
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:26,860
Hey, Komachi.

93
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:27,700
Shut up!

94
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:54,840
A straight road that goes on forever.

95
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:58,940
Could you stop with the
endless horizon already?

96
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:03,460
Just thinking about walking down
this road makes my head hurt.

97
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:07,240
I'm fine. The air is fresh,
and there are no cars around.

98
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:10,180
Sorry there are no buses or taxis.

99
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:14,400
Komachi grew up in the countryside, so you're used
to horizons and they don't bother you, I suppose.

100
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:16,800
In Tokyo, you almost never see a horizon.

101
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,180
We city folk get overwhelmed by it.

102
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:22,640
We're not wild, primitive cavemen, you know.

103
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:25,700
You have stamina, but only brute strength.

104
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:27,700
Brute strength has nothing to do with it.

105
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:30,800
I've got explosive power, but no endurance.

106
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:33,840
Besides, the scenery is all the same,
so it gets boring.

107
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:35,960
That's not true.

108
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:38,760
We're surrounded by such rich nature,
after all.

109
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:42,580
Don't birds and the like
get washed away cleanly?

110
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:44,800
Nature exists too, you know.

111
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:46,326
Ryo-chan,

112
00:07:46,376 --> 00:07:50,160
that kind of thinking is what
creates pollution and destroys nature.

113
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:52,880
Reiko, there's no use talking to him.

114
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:55,500
His very existence is pollution itself.

115
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:58,940
Hey, "this guy"? Miss Bamushi,
I don't want to be called that.

116
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:00,000
Miss Scarecrow!

117
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,540
Whatever, damn it. I'm going home.

118
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:06,680
Be my guest.
But it's 30 kilometers to the station.

119
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:09,020
You didn't bring any money, did you?
Only 100 yen.

120
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:11,220
If you can go home, then go ahead and try.

121
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:13,940
Damn it!

122
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:16,780
Hell if we go, hell if we return

123
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:19,280
And there's not a stag beetle in sight.

124
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:21,360
On top of this heat.

125
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:24,620
Damn you, sun!

126
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:30,820
Finally, a turn!

127
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:36,540
Another straight road? Give me a break.

128
00:08:36,540 --> 00:08:37,920
Well, what can you do?

129
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:40,200
The pension's straight down this road, so...

130
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:41,860
There's no way to turn, is there?

131
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:43,840
I'm not sleeping outdoors, you know.

132
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:45,480
Let's hurry up and get to the pension.

133
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:47,460
Enough with the horizon already!

134
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:50,200
Bear with it till the pension, Ryo-chan.

135
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:52,600
What's wrong, Ryo-chan?

136
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:54,920
Every time I hear the word "pension,"

137
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:56,940
the word "pension"...

138
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:58,240
Ugh, how awful.

139
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,500
Let's leave that guy behind and go.

140
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:03,100
Yeah, let him sleep
outside or whatever he wants.

141
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:06,740
Wait, I was joking!
I don't want to sleep outside either.

142
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:20,160
We're really worn out, aren't we?

143
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:23,240
But the stars are so beautiful.

144
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:27,520
And the insects' chirping
is really romantic.

145
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:37,900
You're ruining the mood.

146
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:41,560
There was only one room left, so I reluctantly let
you share it with me, and this is how you repay me?

147
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,400
Now it'll be a little quieter.

148
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:54,160
Yeah, it will.

149
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:55,980
Ryo-chan's asleep too?

150
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:57,160
He seems to be sleeping soundly.

151
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:58,940
He must have been really tired.

152
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:00,520
Then let's get some sleep too.

153
00:10:00,820 --> 00:10:01,480
Okay.

154
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:05,280
It's a stag beetle!

155
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:08,620
Big profits everywhere!

156
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:13,980
It's money, and it's all mine!

157
00:10:21,080 --> 00:10:23,380
Let's rest at this shrine for a bit.

158
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:25,980
We should walk while it's
still cool in the morning.

159
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:27,880
It'll go over 35 degrees
again this afternoon.

160
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:29,200
No way.

161
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:40,020
That's how it is. The day after a day like that gets terribly hot.
That chilly night, I got tossed outside and caught a cold.

162
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:52,560
That Mr. G we passed yesterday...
This is the place from the painting.

163
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:53,760
Mm.

164
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:56,700
Look, this torii gate.

165
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:02,420
So that means it's in that direction...

166
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:04,620
is where this painting was drawn.

167
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:06,180
That's it.

168
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:10,540
Here! This is the place from the painting!

169
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:12,060
No doubt about it!

170
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:13,520
Yes!

171
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:23,240
On my way to deliver a painting
to an elementary school,

172
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:26,003
my car got a flat tire,
and while it was being repaired,

173
00:11:26,053 --> 00:11:28,020
I waited right here in this place.

174
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:34,100
A Frenchman? What do you mean?

175
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,850
I'm not sure, but I definitely remember it.

176
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:40,220
There was a French woman with me.

177
00:11:40,780 --> 00:11:42,500
Wasn't she your mother, Reiko?

178
00:11:43,220 --> 00:11:46,660
No, she's not my mother.
It was someone else.

179
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,020
Well, this adds another mystery.

180
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:53,320
Maybe if we go to the elementary school where we delivered the painting,
you'll remember more details.

181
00:11:54,060 --> 00:11:56,720
We've come all this way,
so let's just go and check it out.

182
00:11:57,220 --> 00:11:57,580
What?

183
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:00,000
Are we still doing this?

184
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,080
We found the place, so isn't that enough?

185
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,200
Anyway, I'm out of breath.

186
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:05,680
Don't complain.

187
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:08,480
You came on this trip to look for Reiko's memories,
didn't you?

188
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:10,900
I just remembered one more thing.

189
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:12,560
The person who painted that picture.

190
00:12:13,020 --> 00:12:14,640
I feel like my heart's
going to come out of my mouth.

191
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:17,740
So he's some famous guy or something?

192
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:19,540
Even we know about him, after all.

193
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:20,720
He's super famous.

194
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:22,620
So his works are expensive?

195
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:26,500
He's a painter famous all over Europe,
part of the Paris school.

196
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:30,040
Of course the new works, but the
old ones also sell for quite a price.

197
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,580
Quite a price, like ten million yen?

198
00:12:32,740 --> 00:12:34,080
About ten times that.

199
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:35,660
A-a hundred million!?

200
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:37,940
So it's an elementary school around here.

201
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:39,840
I heard you talking about money!

202
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:41,080
It'll pay for itself, after all.

203
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:43,400
It's not like it'll become ours, though.

204
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:45,820
There are three of them, but which one?

205
00:12:46,540 --> 00:12:48,180
I can't remember the name.

206
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:50,140
But it's one of these, I'm sure of it.

207
00:12:50,140 --> 00:12:52,180
And the directions are totally different.

208
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:55,940
Fine, it's a one-in-three chance.

209
00:12:57,260 --> 00:12:58,180
Whoa!

210
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:02,580
This is when my country-bred
instincts come in handy!

211
00:13:02,580 --> 00:13:03,540
Count on me, Komachi.

212
00:13:04,440 --> 00:13:05,580
Pick one.

213
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,340
Um...

214
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:11,600
Hot!

215
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:14,620
Yaaah!

216
00:13:27,340 --> 00:13:29,120
One-in-three odds.

217
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:31,880
No, it wasn't like this.

218
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:32,880
Wrong.

219
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:36,420
We walked three hours for nothing?

220
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:40,680
I'm sure there was a big clock in
the middle of the school building.

221
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:45,100
A clock... someone must be there
during summer break. Let's ask.

222
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:46,200
What do you want?

223
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:53,100
Then it's Ipponsugi Elementary School.

224
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:57,640
We're going the completely opposite way. Reiko's instincts aren't worth much,
are they?

225
00:13:57,760 --> 00:13:58,300
What?

226
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:01,580
If you have business there,
you'd better hurry.

227
00:14:02,260 --> 00:14:05,370
Ipponsugi Elementary
School closed this spring,

228
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:09,580
and demolition work is supposed
to begin this afternoon.

229
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:12,200
Hurry, quick, quick.

230
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:13,920
They're really fired up, aren't they.

231
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:15,240
Suspicious.

232
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:17,415
The school is being demolished.

233
00:14:17,465 --> 00:14:20,900
If they're tearing it down,
they won't need the painting, right?

234
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:24,360
Yes! I'll take the 100 million yen!

235
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:27,760
Mine! Mine!

236
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:40,520
They've already started tearing it down!
Damn it!

237
00:14:40,980 --> 00:14:42,040
We have to stop them!

238
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:43,440
Go, Ryotsu!

239
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:47,160
We came all this way, and now we're
just going to lose 100 million yen!

240
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:50,380
Coming! Wait up!

241
00:14:52,420 --> 00:14:53,640
Stop!

242
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:20,800
After all that searching!

243
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:21,520
Shock.

244
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:25,000
I'm fine, so don't worry.

245
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:34,320
Not yet. It's too early to give up.

246
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:35,760
Ryo-chan!

247
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:40,900
Is it a memory you can give up on so easily?

248
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:46,000
If you give up, Reiko,
that memory will vanish for good!

249
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:49,240
And the French woman in the memory,
too, of course.

250
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:51,360
Ryo-chan.

251
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:53,340
Just kidding!

252
00:15:53,340 --> 00:15:57,400
Your scheme is so obvious. You want
to find the painting and make it yours!

253
00:15:57,400 --> 00:16:00,000
Where's my 100 million yen?

254
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:09,260
Nothing to be found.

255
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:13,500
That's enough, Ryo-chan. I give up.

256
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:14,120
Reiko.

257
00:16:16,020 --> 00:16:17,440
Are you looking for something?

258
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:22,660
I worked here as a
janitor until this spring.

259
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:23,460
Yeah.

260
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:30,340
That's refreshing.

261
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:33,260
I remember that painting well.

262
00:16:33,260 --> 00:16:36,720
Well, so you were the
little girl from back then.

263
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:37,163
Yes,

264
00:16:37,213 --> 00:16:41,160
but I wasn't able to be reunited with
the painting of my cherished memory.

265
00:16:41,960 --> 00:16:46,120
Well, if you were asking about that painting,
there's no need to worry.

266
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:48,560
Is the painting somewhere?

267
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:51,940
Where on earth is it?

268
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:55,400
The principal in Sendai
donated it to a museum.

269
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:58,240
The museum, the 100 million yen one,

270
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:01,820
don't mind it. As for that museum,

271
00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:04,640
it's about three hours from here.

272
00:17:04,640 --> 00:17:06,320
We have to walk again?

273
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:09,280
If you like, I'll drive you there.

274
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:10,140
Really?

275
00:17:12,140 --> 00:17:14,480
Why do I have to run alone?

276
00:17:14,860 --> 00:17:17,140
It can't be helped. It's a four-seater.

277
00:17:17,140 --> 00:17:18,740
Yes, we'd be over the limit.

278
00:17:18,740 --> 00:17:19,820
Damn you!

279
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:24,140
Fifteen years ago,
a museum was built in the prefecture,

280
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:27,260
and it collects and displays
art related to the Goto area.

281
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:28,880
Thank goodness.

282
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:33,280
Now Reiko's memories
should surely come back.

283
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:43,040
Inside the museum, be quiet.

284
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:45,720
Easy for you to say,
but you're out of breath

285
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:48,540
This is the painting.

286
00:17:54,980 --> 00:17:56,340
This woman is

287
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:02,740
Yes, this is the painting.

288
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:09,420
So the French woman from my memory
was the woman in this painting.

289
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:16,020
What a beautiful woman.
Doesn't she look a little like Reiko?

290
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:23,900
Hey, that pen is exactly
like the one Reiko is wearing.

291
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:27,360
The color and shape are exactly the same!

292
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:30,940
This was my grandmother's keepsake.

293
00:18:30,940 --> 00:18:33,280
It was something she treasured very much.

294
00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:34,960
So that means maybe...

295
00:18:35,540 --> 00:18:37,760
Is the model for this
painting Reiko's grandma?

296
00:18:38,760 --> 00:18:41,880
Then is the little girl Reiko's mom?

297
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:44,160
Grandmother...

298
00:18:46,220 --> 00:18:47,800
So it was her after all.

299
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:53,460
I'm Mamia Shinzō.

300
00:18:54,180 --> 00:18:55,360
Mamia paints.

301
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:56,080
100 million yen.

302
00:18:56,080 --> 00:18:56,580
Idiot.

303
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:01,940
That countryside scene was
painted by me decades ago.

304
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:07,180
When it appeared in a travel magazine,
I suddenly wanted to visit it again.

305
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:12,080
Meeting you there,
fate really is a strange thing.

306
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:20,520
When I passed you, I couldn't believe it.

307
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:26,300
Looking at you now, your eyes are
just like Madame Albert's back then.

308
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:36,460
Shortly after the war, I set out to
become a painter and went to Paris.

309
00:19:36,460 --> 00:19:40,500
I thought that if I just got to Paris,
all my wishes would come true.

310
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:44,400
But reality was far harsher than I expected.

311
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:48,400
Not a single painting sold,
and my money soon ran out.

312
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:51,240
I was even thrown out of
the apartment I was living in.

313
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:56,040
That was when despair closed in on my heart.

314
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:09,560
Her name was Albert.

315
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:13,800
Before the war, Fujita, who was active then,
was on friendly terms with people like Hiroshi,

316
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:15,780
and she spoke broken Japanese.

317
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:21,360
Albert's family were all kind and
treated me with great consideration.

318
00:20:22,880 --> 00:20:25,700
I felt as at ease as if I were at home,

319
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:35,020
and I was so grateful to everyone. Since I could not repay them in any way,
I poured all my gratitude into

320
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:38,400
a painting of her and her daughter.

321
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:51,280
After I returned to Japan, I was fortunate enough to
gain recognition with works depicting Japanese landscapes.

322
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:54,040
That's true of the one that
appeared in that magazine too.

323
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:59,680
So that painting is what led
you and Reiko all the way here.

324
00:20:59,680 --> 00:21:04,860
Yes, when my paintings finally started selling,
I sent Albert a letter,

325
00:21:05,340 --> 00:21:07,280
but it came back marked as undeliverable.

326
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:11,560
I never heard anything about her since then.

327
00:21:11,960 --> 00:21:15,020
I barely know anything
about my grandmother either.

328
00:21:16,300 --> 00:21:19,900
According to my grandmother's will,
when I became an elementary school student,

329
00:21:22,280 --> 00:21:23,460
I was apparently told that,

330
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:27,560
but I didn't know this
painting was in Japan either.

331
00:21:27,560 --> 00:21:30,240
I suppose it's because I was Japanese

332
00:21:30,240 --> 00:21:31,820
that they told me that, then.

333
00:21:31,820 --> 00:21:32,500
Yes.

334
00:21:33,820 --> 00:21:36,380
If Albert hadn't helped me back then,

335
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:38,640
I wouldn't be who I am today.

336
00:21:43,220 --> 00:21:45,800
He told me,
"If you're ever short on money, sell this."

337
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:47,560
Albert gave it to me.

338
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:56,440
But no matter how hard life got,
I never let go of it.

339
00:21:58,100 --> 00:21:59,660
It belongs to you.

340
00:22:28,780 --> 00:22:30,400
All that hard work was worth it

341
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:34,160
Ryo-chan, thanks for everything.

342
00:22:34,160 --> 00:22:37,660
Stop it,
being told that by you makes me blush.

343
00:22:37,660 --> 00:22:41,140
I'm glad I came along on this
trip to find your memories.

344
00:22:41,600 --> 00:22:41,960
Mm.

345
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:45,620
That painter was single, wasn't he?

346
00:22:45,620 --> 00:22:50,640
So maybe he kept thinking about
Reiko's grandmother all this time.

347
00:22:52,820 --> 00:22:55,020
That's kind of romantic, isn't it?

348
00:22:55,600 --> 00:22:59,380
Everyone has a memory that makes their heart flutter,
that's all.

349
00:23:02,820 --> 00:23:04,200
What's that supposed to mean?

350
00:23:05,360 --> 00:23:07,220
It didn't make us any money, though.

351
00:23:07,220 --> 00:23:09,580
Well, I suppose this trip
was worth it after all.

352
00:23:10,820 --> 00:23:11,780
He's smiling.

353
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:12,740
Creepy.

354
00:23:13,640 --> 00:23:15,240
The bus is here.

355
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:18,640
Alright, everyone, get on, get on!

356
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:52,060
Where are we?

357
00:23:52,820 --> 00:23:56,360
Hey! How on earth do we get back?

358
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:00,020
I'm sick of this horizon!

359
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:20,460
An afternoon after the rain,
as if someone is calling out,

360
00:24:21,720 --> 00:24:27,160
walking while looking back,
held by Mom's hand.

361
00:24:27,160 --> 00:24:34,280
That strange feeling I had back then

362
00:24:34,980 --> 00:24:39,540
So on the tiles of the arcade street,

363
00:24:39,540 --> 00:24:45,380
I tried walking only on the black ones.

364
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:51,500
Everyone was a child once,

365
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:53,620
but they've all forgotten.

366
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:57,620
Politicians with their self-important faces,

367
00:24:57,620 --> 00:25:00,920
and the annoying aunties
who are a thorn in your side,

368
00:25:01,460 --> 00:25:03,920
try turning the corner.

369
00:25:04,740 --> 00:25:06,500
That corner.

370
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:11,720
You can meet everyone, everyone.

371
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:16,480
Over there is Kameari!

372
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:20,200
Well, surely Kameari

373
00:25:21,120 --> 00:25:24,920
Come on, everyone, to our town

374
00:25:25,920 --> 00:25:26,400
Thank you

375
00:25:30,120 --> 00:25:34,040
What's this shaking?
Why is it clinging to me?

376
00:25:34,040 --> 00:25:37,320
Could it be because I knocked
over the temple's gravestone?

377
00:25:37,320 --> 00:25:39,520
Hey, find me a lover

378
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:43,120
So which 12th floor are
we even talking about?

379
00:25:44,280 --> 00:25:47,420
See you on the 12th floor next time, okay?

380
00:25:49,420 --> 00:25:50,920
That place is Kameari

381
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:54,280
Surely Kameari

382
00:25:55,560 --> 00:25:59,260
Come on, everyone, to our town

383
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:00,860
Thank you

